---
title: "Free Tool to Redact Screenshots Online"
description: "Hide emails, phone numbers, names, API keys, passwords and faces in a screenshot before you share it. Runs in your browser. Nothing is uploaded."
faq:
  - q: "Is the Screenshot Redactor free?"
    a: "Yes. It is free to use, with no account, no sign-up, no watermark and no limit on how many screenshots you redact."
  - q: "Is my screenshot uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. The page has no server code. Text reading, face detection and name detection all run in your browser, so the screenshot stays on your device. The only downloads are the AI models, which contain no user data."
  - q: "How accurate is the automatic redaction?"
    a: "It finds most text that is clear and of normal size, but it can miss small, faint or stylized text, and it can hide words that are not private. Look over the whole image before you share it. Click a box to show that item again, and drag on the image to cover anything it missed."
  - q: "Can someone unblur or undo the redaction?"
    a: "No. The download is a new, flat PNG image. Under a black box the pixels are black, and blur and pixelate first shrink the area to a few pixels, so the detail is gone. There is no hidden layer with the original text. For the strongest result, use the Black box style."
  - q: "Which languages can it read?"
    a: "English, Spanish, French, German, Dutch, Chinese (simplified and traditional), Japanese and Thai. Choose the language of the screenshot in the AI bar. The tool always reads English too, because apps mix it in for times, buttons and links."
  - q: "Does it work with phone screenshots?"
    a: "Yes. You can choose a screenshot from your phone, and the tool knows a tall phone screenshot and skips the status bar at the top. On a computer you can also paste a screenshot straight from the clipboard."
  - q: "Can it hide API keys and passwords in a screenshot of code or a terminal?"
    a: "Yes. It finds common API keys and tokens, such as AWS, GitHub, Stripe, Slack and OpenAI keys, plus passwords after words like password=, connection strings, private keys and internal host names."
  - q: "What is the difference between this and the Chat Screenshot Anonymizer?"
    a: "The Screenshot Redactor is for any screen: apps, dashboards, emails, code and settings pages. The Chat Screenshot Anonymizer is made for chat apps. It also finds sender names in the chat header and in colored names above messages, and it can give people fake names."
---

